    "result_text": "The acceptance suite is the whole test suite, and it's green. Here's what I implemented for the optional, region-validated `postcode`:\n\n**Contract / schema**\n- `openapi.yml` \u2014 added an optional `postcode` property to `OwnerFields` (so it flows to both `OwnerFieldsDto` and `OwnerDto`), typed `string` with pattern `^[0-9]{4}$`. Because it's not in `required`, an owner without a postcode stays valid (backward-compatible), and a malformed value like `\"12\"` is rejected as a bean-validation `@Pattern` violation \u2192 400.\n\n**Persistence / model**\n- `Owner.java` \u2014 added the `postcode` field, `@Column`, and getter/setter. MapStruct maps it by name on the way in and out, so it's stored and returned. No new mapper code needed.\n- `RegionPostcodes.java` (new, model) \u2014 the fixed region\u2192range lookup (NSW 2000-2099, VIC 3000-3099, QLD 4000-4099), mirroring the existing `CityRegions` lookup and keeping that class focused on city\u2192region.\n- Added the `postcode` column to all four DB schemas (h2/hsqldb/postgres/mysql). This mattered: the test profile is `hsqldb` with `ddl-auto=none`.\n\n**Validation**\n- `PostcodeValidator.java` (new) \u2014 a static helper mirroring `TelephoneNormalizer`/`EmailNormalizer`: null postcode is accepted; otherwise it derives the region via `CityRegions` and checks the range via `RegionPostcodes`, throwing `InvalidPostcodeException` when out of range. A city with no known region (UNKNOWN \u2192 no range) accepts any 4-digit value.\n- `InvalidPostcodeException.java` (new) \u2014 parallels `InvalidTelephoneException`.\n- `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` \u2014 one call to `PostcodeValidator.validate(city, postcode)`, placed alongside the existing telephone/email normalization.\n- `ExceptionControllerAdvice` \u2014 a handler mapping `InvalidPostcodeException` \u2192 400 with an `errors: [\"postcode\"]` body, matching the existing telephone/email handlers.\n\nEach piece sits in its natural home and reuses the existing lookup, validation, and error-advice patterns rather than duplicating them.",
